The ecological and evolutionary factors that drive the development of the Japanese honeybees' heat-balling technique include the co-evolution with the Giant Asian Hornet, a natural predator of honeybee colonies. The bees' aggressive heat-balling behavior is an evolutionary response to protect their hives from these predators. When a hornet enters a hive, the bees surround it and produce heat by shivering their muscles; this raises the temperature inside the ball to levels that are lethal for the hornet but tolerable for the bees. This remarkable defense mechanism exemplifies the dynamics of co-evolutionary arms races between species.
